{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 34, :comments ["<b>Therefore he called his name Levi.</b> It refers back to the Holy One, blessed be He, and therefore it does not say 'she called' (קראה), for He, blessed be He, is the one who calls and unites him to His service, for he is the tenth among the sons, and the tenth is consecrated to HaShem. And since she knew with certainty that there would be at least three sons in her portion, therefore she said, 'for I have borne him three sons,' and her intention in it corresponds to the conjunction of the three above, which are inscribed in the word איש, as hinted in Sefer ha-Bahir. And this is the meaning of ילוה אישי — for the three of them are one conjunction and one matter. And for this reason the sons of Levi were the teachers of Torah and wisdom, as is said (Deuteronomy 33:10): ״יורו משפטיך ליעקב״. And the tribe of Levi was divided into two families: priesthood and Levitical service. And when she bore the fourth, Judah, who was beyond her portion, she wished to specify what had already been included in the name Levi, and she called his name Judah — that is, she gave acknowledgment (הודאה) to Binah, which is the place of the sons, and she said, ״הפעם אודה את ה׳״. And from this one learns that everything is included in the four sons of Leah: Reuben and Simeon are an allusion to the Shekhinah with its six extremities; Levi and Judah are an allusion to the three that ride upon them."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:16:25.184443459Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}